+
+## AIOS local CDP transport (development)
+
+On Unix hosts, `--cdp unix:/absolute/socket/path` discovers and connects to a browser through a filesystem Unix socket. Linux and Android additionally accept `--cdp abstract:chrome_devtools_remote`. The process must already have OS permission to connect. This option does not grant privileges, launch Chrome, use ADB, or fall back to TCP. Discovery ignores the advertised host and keeps the connection on the selected socket. HTTP discovery and WebSocket handshakes have five-second deadlines; discovery is capped at 64 KiB and requires an uncompressed, non-chunked HTTP response. Local WebSocket messages are capped at 32 MiB. Custom CDP headers are not accepted on this transport.
+
+This downstream transport is under development and has not been validated on Android Chrome. Existing `connect` and `--cdp` command parsing is reused. AIOS uses its own Binder interface, not the upstream MCP server. The upstream MCP wrapper inherits this endpoint through the same CLI configuration; it has no separate local-socket tool or permission bypass.
